  "live_records": {
    "q-158527": {
      "dom_id": "step-q158527",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}^{2}\\le 6{x}_{1}^{2},\\,4{x}_{3}^{2}\\le 6{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 6{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+6{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =6({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=6(1)=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 6.$ \n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $6.$ But before we conclude that $6$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=6.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3(0)^{2}+6(1)^{2}+4(0)^{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of  $Q(x)$ is equal to $6.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
