    "q-158526": {
      "dom_id": "step-q158526",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-5{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{2}^{2}\\le 2{x}_{2}^{2},\\,-5{x}_{3}^{2}\\le 2{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=2(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 2.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $2.$ But before we conclude that $2$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=2.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(1)^{2}-3(0)^{2}-5(0)^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
