    "q-138065": {
      "dom_id": "step-q138065",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=-3{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge -4{x}_{1}^{2},\\,5{x}_{3}^{2}\\ge -4{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-3{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -4{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=-4(1)=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -4.$   \n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-4.$ But before we conclude that $-4$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-4.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-3{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-3(0)^{2}-4(1)^{2}+5(0)^{2} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
