    "q-158530": {
      "dom_id": "step-q158530",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=-2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-2{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge -5{x}_{1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -5{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-5({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=-5(1)=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -5.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-5.$ But before we conclude that $-5$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-5.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-2(0)^{2}-5(1)^{2} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-5.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
