    "q-136824": {
      "dom_id": "step-q136824",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge -3{x}_{1}^{2},\\,-{x}_{3}^{2}\\ge -3{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -3{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=-3(1)=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -3.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-3.$ But before we conclude that $-3$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-3.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(0)^{2}-3(1)^{2}-(0)^{2} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
